# CrossLayer adapter: @@TITLE@@

Generated by `crosslayer new adapter @@NAME@@`. It already works on
`examples/sample.log`:

```sh
pip install -e .            # needs crosslayer installed in the same environment
pytest                      # conformance, contract tests, lint, sample run
crosslayer run experiments/analyze_sample.yaml
```

## Adapting it to your system

1. **Parse your logs.** Edit `LINE`, `FIELD`, and `ID_FIELDS` in
   `@@NAME@@/adapter.py`. Fields named in `ID_FIELDS` become correlation ids;
   everything else becomes a namespaced attribute (`@@NAME@@.<field>`).
2. **Describe your system.** Set `subsystems`, `interfaces`, and
   `system_version` in `describe()`.
3. **Declare your events.** Update `event_catalog()`. `pytest` checks that
   real output stays within it, and `crosslayer lint` uses it to catch
   misspelled event types and attributes in contracts.
4. **Write contracts** in `@@NAME@@/contracts.yaml`, owned by the team
   responsible for each boundary.
5. **Prove each contract can fail.** Add a known-good and a known-bad case per
   contract to `@@NAME@@/contract_tests.yaml`.
6. **Check:**
   ```sh
   pytest
   crosslayer lint experiments/analyze_sample.yaml --strict
   crosslayer test-contracts @@NAME@@/contract_tests.yaml --require-coverage
   ```

To let CrossLayer launch the system instead of only reading its logs,
implement `build()`/`execute()` and declare `Capability.BUILD`/`Capability.EXECUTE`.
